Challenge: Independent vs. Aliased Handles
Using the Packet class from this article, write a small testbench that creates one object pkt with count = 5. Then create a second handle pkt_alias that points to the same object as pkt, and a third handle pkt_copy that points to a brand new, independent object also constructed with count = 5. Change pkt.count to 99, then display count through all three handles to confirm which ones change


/*
Challenge: Independent vs. Aliased Handles
Using the Packet class from this article, write a small testbench that creates one object pkt with count = 5. Then create a second handle pkt_alias that points to the same object as pkt, and a third handle pkt_copy that points to a brand new, independent object also constructed with count = 5. Change pkt.count to 99, then display count through all three handles to confirm which ones changed.
  */
  
class packet;
  int count;
  string name;
  
  function new(int c);
    count =c;
  endfunction
  
  function void display(string name);
    $display("name=%s count=%0d",name, count);
  endfunction
endclass

module tab;
  packet pac, pac_alias, pkt_copy;
  
  initial begin
    pac=new(5);
    pac.display("display_pac");

    pac_alias=pac;
    pac_alias.display("pac_alias_of_pac");

    pkt_copy=new(5);
    pkt_copy.display("pkt_copy");

    pac.count=99;
    pac.display("pac_value override");
  end
endmodule 
    
